From 14d203d3f47a833436065107bc172b8a6d2001f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michiel <michieleghuizen@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 13:21:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 03/14] lsm: add inode_getattr_rcu, the rcu-walk counterpart of
 inode_getattr

stat() is the one path-based syscall whose operation does not need the
object pinned: it reads a few dozen bytes out of an inode that RCU
already keeps in memory. The VFS still leaves rcu-walk at the end of
every stat, because the only way it has to hand a struct path to
security_inode_getattr() and ->getattr() is with references taken. On a
dentry many CPUs stat at once - a shared library, a directory a build
tree stats a thousand times - those two atomic operations on the
dentry's cacheline are the whole cost of the syscall that scales.

A later patch lets the VFS answer a stat without leaving rcu-walk when
the filesystem says its ->getattr can run there. That needs the LSM
check to run there too. This adds the hook for it.

inode_getattr_rcu takes the same struct path as inode_getattr, but is
called under rcu_read_lock() with nothing pinned: the dentry and mount
are RCU-protected memory whose contents may be changing under the hook,
and the VFS discards the whole result if the dentry's sequence count
moved. The hook may not sleep and may not take a reference it cannot
drop without sleeping. It returns 0 to allow, -ECHILD if it cannot
decide under those rules - the VFS then redoes the stat in ref-walk and
asks inode_getattr - or another error to deny. This is the same contract
inode_permission has had with MAY_NOT_BLOCK, and inode_follow_link with
its rcu argument, for years; SELinux already answers both from RCU.

Because the default return is 0 and call_int_hook() stops at the first
non-zero answer, every active LSM that provides the hook sees the call
and any of them can deny or defer.

An LSM that hooks inode_getattr without providing inode_getattr_rcu
would be bypassed by a stat answered in rcu-walk. security_add_hooks()
detects that at registration and disables the rcu variant for the whole
system with a static key, so the VFS falls back to ref-walk exactly as
today. SELinux gets an implementation in the next patch; AppArmor,
Smack and TOMOYO hook inode_getattr and do not provide the variant, so
on kernels running them nothing changes until they do.

The BPF LSM registers a stub for every hook, including this one, and
programs attach to those stubs. A program attached to inode_getattr may
be sleepable, and in any case must see every stat, so the BPF LSM's
entry for inode_getattr_rcu is a gate: while anything is attached to
inode_getattr it returns -ECHILD, otherwise it calls the real stub,
which programs may attach to in turn. Those programs run under RCU, and
the verifier already refuses sleepable programs on hooks not listed as
sleepable, which this one is not. The trampoline code maintains the
attachment count, incrementing before the hook's text is patched on
attach and decrementing after it is restored on detach, so there is no
window in which an attached program is skipped.

No caller yet; that is the next patches.
